  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Ticket Rail",
    "overview": "Ion turns a request into a ticket and keeps every ticket on a live rail until it is settled. The home is a question set in mono (\"What should we build in waku?\"), one composer under it, and a grid of ticket cards rising out of a mint-to-lime glow field at the bottom of the window. Everything else (Decisions, Teams, Routines, Workflows, Artifacts, Apps, Harnesses) is a Paperclip-like workspace in a quiet sidebar: an org canvas for teams, pipelines for workflows, rows for routines, cards with real brand marks for apps.\n\nLime `#c2ef68` is the signal, not the paint. It fills the one action that moves work forward (send, Allow, an enabled switch) and outlines the ticket that needs you. Broad surfaces stay neutral green-grey in both themes; light and dark are designed with equal care and share one token schema.\n\nThree clients, one vocabulary:\n\n- **Desktop** (Rust/GPUI): compact chrome, `sp()` scaled type, the full workspace. Tokens in `src/theme.rs`; shared parts in `src/ui/`.\n- **Android** (Expo/React Native): the same palette mirrored in `apps/mobile/src/constants/theme.ts`, with 36-48dp touch targets and larger type.\n- **Website** (TanStack Start + Tailwind, `website/src/styles.css`): the \"forge\" register. Light only, oversized uppercase Geist display, square 2px CTA blocks and dark \"night\" bands. It reuses the app's ink, canvas, lime, leaf and glow, and shows the product through a hero demo of the composer and rail.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One lime action per context; status keeps its own hue and always a label.",
      "Tickets: mono `#XXXX` number in a stub, a dashed perforation, then title, station and state pill.",
      "Mono for identity and the home question; system sans for everything operational.",
      "Hairline translucent borders for structure; layered soft shadows for elevation.",
      "Pills for controls (chips, segments, toggles, send); 12px corners for objects."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Lime Action Rule",
        "body": "Lime fills at most one control per context: the action that moves this ticket or this prompt forward. Everything beside it is outlined or quiet.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Label Carries Meaning Rule",
        "body": "Every state colour ships with an icon and a word (Needs you, Working, Failed, Ready). Hue is never the only signal; toggles also move their knob.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Glow Is One Field Rule",
        "body": "The mint-to-lime glow is painted once, as a large field behind home (and the site hero). Never as scattered tints on cards.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Mono Means Identity Rule",
        "body": "Mono marks things with an identity (ticket numbers, the home question, `@` sigils, code). It is not used for ordinary labels.",
        "section": "typography"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** give each context at most one lime fill, and make it the action that moves work forward.",
      "**Do** put new live work on the rail as a ticket with a stable mono number and a labelled state.",
      "**Do** design every surface in light and dark from the same tokens; check both.",
      "**Do** use `elevation()` levels (or Android's `elevation*` strings) instead of ad hoc shadows.",
      "**Do** keep desktop chrome type in `sp()` and Android targets at 48dp.",
      "**Do** use bundled or cached real brand marks for apps and providers."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** use lime as light-theme text or as a large surface fill; use leaf `#3d5a12` for text.",
      "**Don't** scatter the glow as tints on cards or sections; it is one field.",
      "**Don't** set ordinary labels in mono or in uppercase tracking inside the apps.",
      "**Don't** add uppercase eyebrow labels above headings on any surface.",
      "**Don't** let a ticket be cut at the rail edge or reorder settled tickets by state.",
      "**Don't** carry the website's 2px blocks and uppercase display into the native apps; the forge register is the site's alone."
    ]
  }
}
